Difference Between UNION/UNION ALL and JOIN
Feature	JOIN	UNION/UNION ALL
Purpose	Combines related data from multiple tables.	Combines results from multiple SELECT queries.
Data Structure	Combines rows horizontally (side by side).	Combines rows vertically (one after another).
Condition	Requires a ON condition to match rows.	No condition; just aligns columns.
Duplicate Removal	N/A (depends on data)	UNION removes duplicates; UNION ALL does not.
UNION vs UNION ALL
UNION

Combines and removes duplicate rows.
Slightly slower due to the extra step of removing duplicates.

SELECT name FROM users
UNION
SELECT name FROM customers;
UNION ALL

Combines all rows, including duplicates.
Faster since it skips duplicate removal.

SELECT name FROM users
UNION ALL
SELECT name FROM customers;
Example with UNION ALL
Table: users
id	name
1	John
2	Jane
Table: customers
id	name
1	Alice
2	John

Query:

SELECT name FROM users
UNION ALL
SELECT name FROM customers;
Result:

name
John
Jane
Alice
John
When to Use UNION/UNION ALL
Use UNION when you want to merge datasets and remove duplicates.
Use UNION ALL when you want to retain all rows, including duplicates
